\title{The Tropical Structure of ReLU Networks:\\
A Forward Correspondence and Quantitative Approximation Bounds}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-06-30}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We give a self-contained, bottom-up development of the \emph{forward direction} of the correspondence between rectified-linear (ReLU) neural networks and tropical rational functions in the min-plus semiring $(\R, \oplus = \min, \otimes = +)$. We define tropical polynomials as finite, nonempty minima of affine functionals and tropical rational functions as their differences, and we prove that this class is closed under the operations a feed-forward ReLU network performs: affine maps, negation, pointwise sum, pointwise maximum, ReLU, scalar multiplication, finite sums, and affine combinations. The central technical step is a \emph{tropical distributive law} --- that the sum of two minima of affine functionals is a minimum over the product index set --- from which closure under maximum, and hence ReLU, follows. As a consequence, every function computed by a feed-forward ReLU network is a tropical rational function. We then develop the quantitative side: the number of tropical monomials is an effective notion of degree controlling both approximation rates for smooth targets and convex/concave expressivity. We give explicit rates --- uniform error $O(N^{-1})$ for Lipschitz targets and $O(N^{-2})$ for targets with a Lipschitz derivative, using an $O(N)$-monomial family --- and formulate three sharp conjectures: a convexity-defect characterization of the polynomial/rational gap, an $N^{-s}$ smoothness staircase, and an alternation-count law for the number of subtractions.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} ReLU networks, tropical geometry, min-plus semiring, tropical rational functions, piecewise-linear approximation, universal approximation, convexity defect, depth--width tradeoffs.

\section{Introduction}

The rectified linear unit $\ReLU(t) = \max(0, t)$ is the dominant nonlinearity in modern neural networks. A feed-forward ReLU network alternates affine maps with coordinatewise applications of $\ReLU$ and therefore computes a continuous \textbf{piecewise-linear} function. A now-classical observation is that the class of such functions coincides with the class of \textbf{tropical rational functions}: differences of tropical polynomials. This places the expressive power of ReLU networks inside tropical geometry, where complexity is measured combinatorially by counting monomials (affine pieces).

This paper is twofold. First, we prove, as an independent tower of lemmas with no circular dependence, that \emph{every} function computed by a feed-forward ReLU network is a tropical rational function. Each lemma is a closure property; the network theorem follows by induction on layers. The only nontrivial ingredient is a distributive law relating the sum of two minima to a single minimum over a product index set. Second, we turn the qualitative dictionary into a quantitative one: we explain why a single tropical polynomial cannot represent functions that bulge above their chords, give explicit approximation rates, and formulate sharp conjectures on the exact cost of expressivity.

Throughout we use the \textbf{min-plus} convention: tropical addition is $\min$, tropical multiplication is ordinary $+$. The max-plus convention is obtained by negation, with $\min \leftrightarrow \max$ and concave $\leftrightarrow$ convex.

\section{Definitions}

Fix $n \in \mathbb{N}$. We work with functions on $\R^n$, indexed by $\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Affine functional]
An \emph{affine functional} is a pair $(a, b)$ with $a \in \R^n$, $b \in \R$, evaluated by
$\mathrm{aff}_{(a,b)}(x) = \inner{a, x} + b = \sum_j a_j x_j + b$.
An affine functional is a \emph{tropical monomial}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical polynomial]
A function $f : \R^n \to \R$ is a \emph{tropical polynomial} if there is a finite, nonempty set $S$ of affine functionals with
\[
f(x) = \min_{(a,b) \in S}\big(\inner{a, x} + b\big) \qquad \text{for all } x.
\]
The cardinality $|S|$ is the \emph{monomial count}. Such an $f$ is the lower envelope of finitely many hyperplanes and is therefore \textbf{concave} and piecewise linear.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical rational function]
A function $f : \R^n \to \R$ is a \emph{tropical rational function} if there exist tropical polynomials $g, h$ with $f(x) = g(x) - h(x)$ for all $x$. We write $\mathcal{R}_n$ for the class of such functions.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[ReLU and ReLU networks]
The \emph{rectifier} is $\ReLU(t) = \max(0, t)$. A \emph{feed-forward ReLU network} is a finite composition of layers, each applying an affine map $z \mapsto Wz + c$ followed by coordinatewise $\ReLU$, with a final affine readout. Each output coordinate is built from input coordinates, constants, affine combinations, and $\ReLU$.
\end{definition}

\section{The Forward Correspondence}

We build $\mathcal{R}_n$ up by closure properties; no statement uses the final network theorem, so the development is non-circular.

\subsection{Base cases}

\begin{lemma}[Affine functionals are tropical polynomials]
For any $a \in \R^n$, $b \in \R$, the function $x \mapsto \inner{a, x} + b$ is a tropical polynomial, witnessed by $S = \{(a, b)\}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $S = \{(a,b)\}$ the minimum is the single value $\inner{a,x} + b$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Constants are tropical polynomials]
For any $c \in \R$, the constant $x \mapsto c$ is a tropical polynomial, witnessed by $S = \{(0, c)\}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Polynomials are rational]
Every tropical polynomial is a tropical rational function.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$f = f - 0$, where $0$ is a tropical polynomial.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The tropical distributive law}

\begin{lemma}[Distributive law for minima]\label{lem:dist}
Let $S, T$ be finite nonempty sets and $u : S \to \R$, $v : T \to \R$. Then
\[
\min_{s \in S} u(s) + \min_{t \in T} v(t) = \min_{(s,t) \in S \times T}\big(u(s) + v(t)\big).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
($\leq$) For all $s, t$, $\min_S u + \min_T v \le u(s) + v(t)$, so the left side lower-bounds every term of the right. ($\ge$) Choosing minimizers $s^\star, t^\star$, the term $u(s^\star) + v(t^\star)$ equals the left side and is at least the right-side minimum. Equality follows.
\end{proof}

In tropical language, Lemma~\ref{lem:dist} states that $\otimes$ distributes over $\oplus$.

\subsection{Closure of tropical polynomials}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under pointwise sum]\label{lem:polyadd}
If $f, g$ are tropical polynomials, so is $x \mapsto f(x) + g(x)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $f = \min_{(a,b)\in S}(\inner{a,x}+b)$ and $g = \min_{(c,d)\in T}(\inner{c,x}+d)$, Lemma~\ref{lem:dist} gives
$f(x) + g(x) = \min_{S\times T}(\inner{a+c, x} + (b+d))$, a tropical polynomial.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under pointwise minimum]\label{lem:polymin}
If $f, g$ are tropical polynomials, so is $x \mapsto \min(f(x), g(x))$, witnessed by the union of the two affine families.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under nonnegative scaling]\label{lem:polyscale}
If $f$ is a tropical polynomial and $c \ge 0$, then $x \mapsto c\,f(x)$ is a tropical polynomial.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For $c \ge 0$, multiplication by $c$ commutes with $\min$: $c\min_{(a,b)\in S}(\inner{a,x}+b) = \min_{(a,b)\in S}(\inner{ca,x}+cb)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Closure of tropical rational functions}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under negation]
If $f \in \mathcal{R}_n$ then $-f \in \mathcal{R}_n$: if $f = g - h$ then $-f = h - g$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under sum]\label{lem:ratadd}
If $f_1, f_2 \in \mathcal{R}_n$ then $f_1 + f_2 \in \mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $f_i = g_i - h_i$, $f_1 + f_2 = (g_1 + g_2) - (h_1 + h_2)$, using Lemma~\ref{lem:polyadd}.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under maximum]\label{lem:ratmax}
If $f_1, f_2 \in \mathcal{R}_n$ then $x \mapsto \max(f_1(x), f_2(x)) \in \mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Write $f_i = g_i - h_i$, $A = g_1 + h_2$, $B = g_2 + h_1$ (tropical polynomials by Lemma~\ref{lem:polyadd}). Using $\max(p,q) = (p+q) - \min(p,q)$,
\[
\max(f_1, f_2) = (A + B) - \big[\min(A, B) + (h_1 + h_2)\big].
\]
The numerator is a tropical polynomial (Lemma~\ref{lem:polyadd}); the bracket is a tropical polynomial (Lemmas~\ref{lem:polymin},~\ref{lem:polyadd}).
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under scalar multiplication]\label{lem:ratscale}
If $f \in \mathcal{R}_n$ and $c \in \R$, then $c\,f \in \mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
With $f = g - h$: if $c \ge 0$, $cf = (cg) - (ch)$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:polyscale}; if $c < 0$, set $d = -c > 0$ and $cf = (dh) - (dg)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under ReLU]\label{lem:relu}
If $f \in \mathcal{R}_n$ then $\ReLU(f) = \max(0, f) \in \mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The constant $0 \in \mathcal{R}_n$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:ratmax} to $0$ and $f$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Closure under affine combinations]\label{lem:affcomb}
If $f_1, \dots, f_m \in \mathcal{R}_n$ and $w_1, \dots, w_m, b \in \R$, then $x \mapsto \sum_i w_i f_i(x) + b \in \mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Each $w_i f_i \in \mathcal{R}_n$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:ratscale}; the constant $b \in \mathcal{R}_n$; close under the sum by Lemma~\ref{lem:ratadd}.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The network theorem}

\begin{theorem}[ReLU networks are tropical rational functions]\label{thm:main}
Every output coordinate of a feed-forward ReLU network with input dimension $n$ is a tropical rational function in the min-plus semiring.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By induction on layers. Input coordinates $x_j = \inner{e_j, x}$ are affine, hence in $\mathcal{R}_n$. An affine map produces affine combinations (Lemma~\ref{lem:affcomb}); coordinatewise $\ReLU$ preserves $\mathcal{R}_n$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:relu}); the final readout is affine (Lemma~\ref{lem:affcomb}). Hence every output coordinate is in $\mathcal{R}_n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Tightness]
The converse holds too: $\min(p,q) = -\max(-p,-q)$ and $\max(p,q) = \ReLU(p-q) + q$, so every tropical rational function is ReLU-computable. The ReLU-computable functions \emph{equal} the tropical rational functions; Theorem~\ref{thm:main} is the forward half.
\end{remark}

\section{Monomial Count as Effective Degree}

For $f = g - h$ with $g = \min_S$, $h = \min_T$, the \emph{monomial count} is $|S| + |T|$; the \emph{tropical rational complexity} is its minimum over representations. This is the right yardstick for depth--width tradeoffs: the number of linear regions, hence monomial count, grows polynomially in width but can grow \emph{exponentially} in depth. A depth-$d$ network can compute functions whose depth-$(d-1)$ representation needs exponentially many monomials --- exponential width.

\section{Quantitative Approximation Bounds}

\begin{theorem}[Lipschitz rate]\label{thm:lip}
Let $g : [0,1] \to \R$ be $L$-Lipschitz. There is an explicit tropical rational function with $O(N)$ monomials with
\[
\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |g(x) - f_N(x)| \le \frac{L}{2N} = O(N^{-1}).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Let $f_N$ be the piecewise-linear interpolant of $g$ at nodes $k/N$; it is tropical rational with $O(N)$ pieces. On each subinterval the interpolation error of an $L$-Lipschitz function is at most $L/(2N)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Smooth rate]\label{thm:smooth}
Let $g : [0,1] \to \R$ have an $M$-Lipschitz derivative. The same $O(N)$-monomial family satisfies
\[
\sup_{x \in [0,1]} |g(x) - f_N(x)| \le \frac{M}{8 N^2} = O(N^{-2}).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
On each subinterval of length $1/N$, linear interpolation of a $C^{1,1}$ function has error at most $\tfrac{M}{8}(1/N)^2$.
\end{proof}

\section{Convexity and the Polynomial/Rational Gap}

\begin{proposition}[Concavity barrier]\label{prop:concave}
A tropical polynomial is concave; hence it cannot equal a function lying strictly below a chord between two of its points.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A minimum of affine (concave) functions is concave.
\end{proof}

\begin{example}[The tent needs a subtraction]
The tent $T(x) = \max(0, 1 - |2x - 1|)$ on $[0,1]$ bulges below the chord joining its endpoints and is not concave, so by Proposition~\ref{prop:concave} it is not a tropical polynomial. It is tropical rational; one subtraction injects exactly the single curvature change the polynomial class cannot supply.
\end{example}

\section{Conjectures and Future Directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Convexity defect is the exact currency of the gap]
For continuous $g$ on $[0,1]$, the smallest uniform error achievable by any finite maximum of affine functions equals half the maximal concavity defect of $g$ --- the largest gap by which $g$ rises above a chord between two of its points --- vanishing precisely when $g$ is convex.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[Monomial count controls the rate at every smoothness order]
A max-plus rational function with $N$ monomials approximates a target with $s$ bounded derivatives on $[0,1]$ to uniform error of order $N^{-s}$, and this exponent is optimal.
\end{conjecture}

\begin{conjecture}[One subtraction per concave bump]
A function on $[0,1]$ is exactly representable as a difference of two max-plus polynomials with $k$ total linear pieces if and only if its graph has at most $k-1$ alternations between convex and concave behavior; one subtraction suffices exactly for unimodal piecewise-linear targets.
\end{conjecture}

\section{Discussion}

The forward correspondence recasts neural-network expressiveness as tropical rational complexity, where the relevant invariant --- monomial count --- is concrete. Depth--width separations become statements about how the number of affine pieces grows under composition, and universal approximation becomes the density of piecewise-linear functions with explicit rates. The proof is robust to conventions and architecture because every layer applies only operations under which $\mathcal{R}_n$ is closed; the lone nontrivial step is the distributive law (Lemma~\ref{lem:dist}). The open frontier is quantitative, captured by the three conjectures above, each anchored to a result proved here.

\section{Conclusion}

Every feed-forward ReLU network computes a tropical rational function in the min-plus semiring, by elementary closure properties resting on the tropical distributive law. Explicit approximation rates and a structural account of why subtraction is necessary complete the dictionary and motivate three sharp conjectures on the quantitative cost of expressivity.

\end{document}

